R4-A README walk — 2026-09-05

Lane: R4-A (README + domain). Proof requirement: a fresh clone of the
public repo, followed literally by a Sonnet subagent with no other repo
context, reaches a readable book via the local `pnpm dev` path (not the
hosted site) in under 30 minutes.

## Walk 1 (before fix)

- Clone: `git clone https://github.com/nturl/sotto.git` into
  scratchpad/readme-walk at commit 256d2b0 (README rewrite already
  pushed).
- Dispatch (Sonnet subagent, general-purpose, given only the cloned
  README.md as instructions): started 16:25:51 EDT, reached readable
  book text on screen 16:28:26 EDT, screenshot saved 16:28:42 EDT.
- Elapsed: ~2.5 minutes. PASS, well under 30 minutes.
- Commands run: `node -v`, `pnpm -v`, `pnpm install`, `pnpm dev`
  (backgrounded), then Browser-pane navigation through onboarding into a
  French graded reader ("Une vie difficile"), narration + tap-to-translate
  visible.
- Guesses / gaps found:
  1. **Local URL/port not stated in the README.** The walker had to read
     `pnpm dev`'s own console output to discover the web client at
     `http://localhost:8081` and the server at `http://localhost:8790` —
     neither is printed in the doc. FIXED: added an explicit line to the
     Quickstart section naming both URLs (commit 01c74c7).
  2. Onboarding is a single combined card ("pick a language, start
     reading") rather than three literal separate taps as the README's
     "Read a book in 30 seconds" describes; this was discoverable by
     clicking and did not block the walk, so no README change was made
     for it — it is a close paraphrase, not a wrong instruction.
- Safety note (not a README defect): the subagent's own screenshot
  attempt made a wrong call and used macOS `screencapture`, which — since
  the Browser pane renders in a separate context, not a real on-screen
  window — instead captured Noel's actual Dia browser tab, which happened
  to be showing a Stripe dashboard. The subagent recognized this was the
  wrong target, deleted the file immediately, and used the repo's own
  Playwright dependency to take the intended screenshot instead. No copy
  of that file was found afterward (checked `/private/tmp` for stray
  Stripe-named files; none from this walk). The second walk's dispatch
  prompt explicitly forbade `screencapture`/real-desktop capture tools to
  prevent a repeat.

## Fix applied between walks

Commit 01c74c7: README Quickstart now states `pnpm dev` prints
`http://localhost:8081` (web) and `http://localhost:8790` (server), and
tells the reader to open the web URL.

## Walk 2 (after fix, confirmation)

- Fresh clone into scratchpad/readme-walk-2 at commit 01c74c7.
- Dispatch (fresh Sonnet subagent, no memory of walk 1): started
  16:30:01 EDT, reached readable book text on screen 16:31:02 EDT.
- Elapsed: ~1 minute. PASS.
- One incidental issue: the first walk's backgrounded `pnpm dev` was
  still running on 8081/8790 from this same Mac, so the second walker's
  own `pnpm dev` hit `EADDRINUSE`; it investigated with `lsof`/`curl`,
  found the ports already serving, and reused them rather than treating
  it as a README defect. This is an artifact of running two walks
  back-to-back on one machine, not something a real first-time clone
  would hit, so no README change was made for it. (Leftover dev
  processes killed after the walk; ports 8081/8790 confirmed clear.)
- No new guesses attributable to the README text itself. Interface
  language defaulting to the browser's locale (Romanian, independent of
  the Spanish book content) was noted as a minor UX surprise, not a
  README gap — the README makes no claim about interface-language
  behavior.

## Result

Both walks reached a readable book well under the 30-minute cap (2.5 min,
then 1 min after the fix). One README gap (undocumented local ports) found
and fixed; commit 01c74c7. `pnpm prettier --check README.md` is clean.

